@page "/how-it-works"
@* How the game is built: the lab, what happens when you play, how a wave is
   judged, the system design and the technology. The tables of containers and
   waves are read from the running lab and the scenario, not written by hand. *@
@using Game.Orchestration
@inject GameHost Game
@inject LabOrchestrator Lab

<div class="doc-page">
    <header>
        <a class="title" href="">@Game.Theme.Label("game")</a>
        <nav class="topnav">
            <a href="">Play</a>
            <a class="active" href="how-it-works">How it's built</a>
        </nav>
    </header>

    <div class="doc">
        <nav class="doc-menu" aria-label="Contents">
            <strong>How it's built</strong>
            <ol>
                @foreach (var (id, title) in Sections)
                {
                    <li><a href="how-it-works#@id">@title</a></li>
                }
            </ol>
            <a class="back" href="">&larr; Back to the game</a>
        </nav>

        <article>
            <section id="overview">
                <h1>How Azeroth SOC is built</h1>
                <p class="lead">
                    Nothing in this game is simulated. When you press Start, the game builds a small network of
                    real Linux containers on your machine. You get a root shell in one of them, the gate, and defend
                    the others with the same tools you would use on a real router: <code>ip</code>, <code>ping</code>,
                    <code>curl</code>, <code>tcpdump</code> and <code>nft</code>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    When you ring the bell, the game does not read your rules. It sends real traffic from the other
                    containers through your gate and measures what gets through. A wave is held when the right
                    people get in and the wrong ones do not, however you wrote the rules.
                </p>
                <div class="facts">
                    <div><span class="big">@(_run?.Spec.Nodes.Count ?? 6)</span> containers</div>
                    <div><span class="big">2</span> isolated networks</div>
                    <div><span class="big">@_scenario.Waves.Length</span> waves</div>
                    <div><span class="big">0</span> simulated packets</div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="lab">
                <h2>The lab: real containers</h2>
                <p>
                    The lab is two networks with the gate between them. On the left are the hosts that come from
                    outside, on the right is the town with its notice board, a web server. Every packet from the
                    plains to the board has to pass through the gate, so the gate is where you defend.
                </p>

                <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 760 300" role="img" aria-label="The lab: two networks with the gate between them">
                    <rect x="10" y="20" width="300" height="260" rx="10" class="net" />
                    <g><text x="24" y="44" class="net-title">@Game.Theme.Label("plains")</text></g>
                    <g><text x="24" y="62" class="mono dim">@Subnet(SentinelHill.Plains)</text></g>
                    @for (var i = 0; i < PlainsRoles.Length; i++)
                    {
                        var role = PlainsRoles[i];
                        var y = 80 + i * 48;
                        <g class="@(role is "defias" or "probe" ? "host bad" : "host")">
                            <rect x="30" y="@y" width="260" height="38" rx="6" />
                            <text x="44" y="@(y + 16)" class="label">@RoleName(role)</text>
                            <text x="44" y="@(y + 31)" class="mono dim">@Address(role, SentinelHill.Plains)</text>
                            <text x="276" y="@(y + 24)" class="mono dim" text-anchor="end">@role</text>
                        </g>
                    }

                    <rect x="330" y="105" width="100" height="90" rx="10" class="gate" />
                    <g><text x="380" y="135" class="label" text-anchor="middle">@Game.Theme.Label("gate")</text></g>
                    <g><text x="380" y="153" class="mono dim" text-anchor="middle">you are root</text></g>
                    <g><text x="380" y="168" class="mono dim" text-anchor="middle">nftables</text></g>
                    <g><text x="380" y="183" class="mono dim" text-anchor="middle">routes</text></g>
                    <line x1="310" y1="150" x2="330" y2="150" class="wire" />
                    <line x1="430" y1="150" x2="450" y2="150" class="wire" />
                    <g><text x="380" y="214" class="mono tiny" text-anchor="middle">&larr; @Address("gate", SentinelHill.Plains)</text></g>
                    <g><text x="380" y="230" class="mono tiny" text-anchor="middle">@Address("gate", SentinelHill.Dmz) &rarr;</text></g>

                    <rect x="450" y="20" width="300" height="260" rx="10" class="net" />
                    <g><text x="464" y="44" class="net-title">@Game.Theme.Label("town")</text></g>
                    <g><text x="464" y="62" class="mono dim">@Subnet(SentinelHill.Dmz)</text></g>
                    <rect x="470" y="80" width="260" height="180" rx="6" class="host board" />
                    <g><text x="484" y="100" class="label">@Game.Theme.Label("board")</text></g>
                    <g><text x="484" y="116" class="mono dim">@Address("web", SentinelHill.Dmz)  nginx</text></g>
                    @for (var i = 0; i < Doors.Length; i++)
                    {
                        var (key, what, open) = Doors[i];
                        var y = 132 + i * 30;
                        <g class="@(open ? "door open" : "door")">
                            <rect x="484" y="@y" width="232" height="24" rx="4" />
                            <text x="494" y="@(y + 16)" class="mono">@Port(key)</text>
                            <text x="560" y="@(y + 16)" class="small-label">@what</text>
                        </g>
                    }
                </svg>
                <p class="caption">
                    @if (_run is not null)
                    {
                        <span>Drawn from the lab that is running now (run <code>@_run.RunId</code>, seed @_run.Spec.Seed).</span>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <span>No lab is running, so this is an example (seed 1). Every run draws new networks and ports from its seed.</span>
                    }
                    Green doors are meant for everyone; the others should stay shut to the outside.
                </p>

                <h3>Who lives in the lab</h3>
                <table class="doc-table">
                    <tr><th>Role</th><th>Name in this theme</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>gate</code></td><td>@Game.Theme.Label("gate")</td><td>Your router and firewall. It forwards between the networks, and you have a root shell in it.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>web</code></td><td>@Game.Theme.Label("board")</td><td>nginx with the public notice board, plus doors that should not be public: SSH for management, a debug page and a database port.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>citizens</code></td><td>@RoleName("citizens")</td><td>Ordinary users. They must always be able to read the board.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>garrison</code></td><td>@RoleName("garrison")</td><td>The administrators. Only they may use the management door.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>defias</code></td><td>@RoleName("defias")</td><td>The attacker: scans, tries the forbidden doors, floods the board.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>probe</code></td><td>@RoleName("probe")</td><td>A second outsider. It takes over the flood halfway, so a block list with one address is not enough.</td></tr>
                </table>

                <h3>The containers right now</h3>
                @if (_dockerError is not null)
                {
                    <p class="muted">Could not ask Docker: @_dockerError</p>
                }
                else if (_run is null)
                {
                    <p class="muted">No lab is running. Press Start in the game, then come back: this table then lists the real containers, read from Docker.</p>
                }
                else
                {
                    <p>Read from Docker when this page was opened. Each container has an image of its own, fixed limits and only the capabilities it needs.</p>
                    <div class="table-wrap">
                        <table class="doc-table mono-table">
                            <tr><th>Container</th><th>Image</th><th>Addresses</th><th>Capabilities</th><th>Limits</th><th>State</th></tr>
                            @foreach (var node in _run.Spec.Nodes)
                            {
                                var live = _run.Nodes.FirstOrDefault(n => n.Role == node.Role);
                                <tr>
                                    <td>@_run.ContainerName(node.Role)</td>
                                    <td>@node.Image</td>
                                    <td class="wrap">@string.Join(", ", node.Attachments.Select(a => a.Address))</td>
                                    <td class="wrap">@(node.CapAdd.Count == 0 ? "none" : string.Join(", ", node.CapAdd))</td>
                                    <td>@(node.MemoryBytes / 1024 / 1024) MB, @node.Cpus CPU, @node.PidsLimit pids</td>
                                    <td class="@(live?.State == "running" ? "good" : "bad")">@(live?.State ?? "missing")</td>
                                </tr>
                            }
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p class="muted small">Try it yourself in a WSL terminal: <code>docker ps --filter label=azeroth.run=@_run.RunId</code></p>
                }
            </section>

            <section id="play">
                <h2>What happens when you play</h2>
                <ol class="steps">
                    <li><strong>You press Start.</strong> The game master in the web server picks a seed. The seed decides
                        everything random in the run: the networks, the addresses, the ports and the word of the day.</li>
                    <li><strong>The lab is built.</strong> The orchestrator asks Docker for two internal networks and six
                        containers, labels them all with the run id and the seed, and adds the routes through the gate.
                        It waits until the citizens can reach the board.</li>
                    <li><strong>The terminal opens.</strong> Your browser connects to the web server over a WebSocket, and
                        the web server opens a <code>bash</code> in the gate through Docker. What you type goes straight to
                        that shell.</li>
                    <li><strong>You talk to the game with <code>quest</code>.</strong> The gate has no network path to the
                        game. <code>quest</code> leaves a request in a file in <code>/run/quest</code>; the game master
                        picks it up from outside with <code>docker exec</code> and writes the answer back.</li>
                    <li><strong>You report or ring the bell.</strong> In a report wave you answer a question with
                        <code>quest report key=value</code>. In a traffic wave you set up the gate and type
                        <code>quest ready</code>, and the attack comes.</li>
                    <li><strong>The wave is judged</strong> by measurement (see the next section). Held: you get XP and the
                        next mission. Not held: you see what fell, adjust the gate and ring again.</li>
                    <li><strong>Everything is saved</strong> in a history file, so the start page can show which skills you
                        have and suggest where to practise next.</li>
                </ol>

                <h3>The waves</h3>
                <table class="doc-table">
                    <tr><th>Wave</th><th>Title</th><th>Skill</th><th>Judged by</th><th class="num">XP</th></tr>
                    @foreach (var wave in _scenario.Waves.OrderBy(w => w.Number))
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td>@wave.Number</td>
                            <td>@wave.Title</td>
                            <td>@wave.Skill</td>
                            <td>@(wave.Kind == WaveSpec.Traffic ? $"traffic, {wave.Checks?.Length ?? 0} check{(wave.Checks?.Length == 1 ? "" : "s")}" : "your report")</td>
                            <td class="num">@wave.Xp</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </table>
                <p class="muted small">
                    A held wave gives its XP minus 20 per hint and 10 per failed try, at least @GameSession.MinimumXp.
                    It is mastered when it was held without hints. With cheat mode on, a wave gives @GameSession.MinimumXp XP.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="judging">
                <h2>How a wave is judged</h2>
                <p>
                    The rule of the game is <em>judge behaviour, not syntax</em>. The game never decides from the text of
                    your rules whether they are right. It reads them only to give hints and to show the state of the
                    gate.
                </p>
                <div class="two-col">
                    <div class="card">
                        <strong>Report waves</strong>
                        <p>You find something out and report it, for example the gate's address or who is talking to the
                            board. The report is compared with the true value of this run. A wrong report tells you which
                            part is wrong, never what the right answer is.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card">
                        <strong>Traffic waves</strong>
                        <p>Each check sends real traffic from the hosts on the plains and counts what is answered: can the
                            citizens read the board, can an outsider reach a forbidden door, how fast does a flood get
                            through.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <h3>Measurements you cannot fake</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Measured where you have no shell.</strong> You are root in the gate, so nothing that decides
                        a wave is measured there. Doors are tried from the plains, and the flood is counted in the web
                        server's access log.</li>
                    <li><strong>Counted, not timed by the clock.</strong> The clock in WSL can jump after Windows has slept,
                        so the traffic scripts count requests and the game measures time with a stopwatch.</li>
                    <li><strong>In doubt, not held.</strong> A measurement that did not go as planned is judged
                        <code>INCONCLUSIVE</code>, which never counts as held.</li>
                    <li><strong>Tested both ways.</strong> Every check is tested against real containers with at least one
                        right defence that must pass and three wrong ones that must fail (<code>Game.Cli selftest</code>).</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    The meters on the game screen come from the same numbers: the health of the town is the lowest share
                    of citizens that were served, and intrusions are the forbidden doors that were reached plus the
                    sources that flooded past the limit. Between waves the game also tries every door every three
                    seconds and lights the lamps on the map.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="design">
                <h2>System design</h2>
                <p>
                    All game logic lives in C# on the server. The page only draws what the game master tells it and
                    sends your clicks back; the terminal is a separate WebSocket straight to the shell in the gate.
                </p>

