                <p class="caption">
                    The proxy sits between the game and Docker. On your machine it is optional; when the game moves to a
                    server it is the only way the game can reach Docker.
                </p>

                <h3>The projects</h3>
                <table class="doc-table">
                    <tr><th>Project</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Game.Api</code></td><td>The web server: the Blazor page, the map, the terminal WebSocket. Draws the game, holds no rules.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Game.Core</code></td><td>The game itself: the game master, waves, hints, scoring, cheat mode, themes and the history.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Game.Checks</code></td><td>The checks that send traffic and measure: which doors answer, how many requests get through, how fast a flood is.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Game.Orchestration</code></td><td>Builds and tears down runs. Its own small Docker client with exactly the calls the lab needs.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Game.DockerProxy</code></td><td>A socket proxy that lets through only those calls, and only for the lab's images, names and addresses.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Game.Cli</code></td><td>Commands for running the lab by hand, playing in a terminal, and every test.</td></tr>
                </table>

                <h3>Design decisions</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>No state file.</strong> A run is a pure function of the scenario and the seed. Everything
                        Docker needs to know is in labels on the containers and networks, so a run can always be rebuilt
                        from them.</li>
                    <li><strong>Data, not code, decides the game.</strong> The waves, their checks and numbers, the hints
                        and the answers are in <code>lab/scenarios/sentinel-hill.json</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Theme and logic apart.</strong> A theme is a JSON file that replaces text only: titles,
                        briefings and names. The game refuses a theme that would change what you have to do. The
                        @Game.Theme.Name theme is in use now.</li>
                    <li><strong>One game master at a time.</strong> The terminal game and the web page both read the same
                        <code>quest</code> files, so only one of them may run a game.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="safety">
                <h2>Keeping the lab safe</h2>
                <p>
                    The lab contains real attack traffic and gives you a root shell, so it is locked in. These are hard
                    requirements in the project, and there are tests for them.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Internal networks.</strong> No container can reach the internet, your home network or the
                        Docker socket. The networks are macvlan on a dummy interface, marked internal.</li>
                    <li><strong>Least privilege.</strong> Only the gate has extra capabilities, <code>NET_ADMIN</code> and
                        <code>NET_RAW</code>, so that you can set rules and run <code>tcpdump</code> and <code>ping</code>.
                        No container is ever privileged.</li>
                    <li><strong>Limits everywhere.</strong> Every container has <code>no-new-privileges</code> and limits on
                        memory, CPU and processes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Attacks only inside the lab.</strong> The attack tools exist only in the lab images and
                        are aimed only at the lab's own addresses in 10.77.0.0/16.</li>
                    <li><strong>Routes from outside.</strong> The routes are added from the host with a privileged
                        <code>ip route replace</code>, the only privileged command the proxy allows.</li>
                    <li><strong>Only from this machine.</strong> The web server listens on 127.0.0.1 only, and the terminal
                        refuses connections from pages on other sites.</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="muted small">Tested by <code>Game.Cli isolation</code>, which tries to get out of the lab from every
                    container, and <code>Game.Cli proxytest</code>, which sends forbidden calls to the proxy.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="tech">
                <h2>Technology</h2>
                <table class="doc-table">
                    <tr><th>Technology</th><th>Used for</th></tr>
                    <tr><td>.NET 10 and C#</td><td>All game logic, the orchestrator, the checks, the proxy and the tests.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>ASP.NET Core (Kestrel)</td><td>The web server and the Docker proxy.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Blazor Server and SignalR</td><td>The game page. The components run on the server and update the browser over SignalR.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>SVG pixel art</td><td>The map, 300 by 104 pixels, drawn in code. No images from other games.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>xterm.js</td><td>The terminal in the browser.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Docker Engine API</td><td>Containers, networks and exec, spoken directly over the socket with a small client of our own.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Docker Desktop on WSL 2</td><td>Runs the lab on Windows.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Alpine Linux</td><td>The base of all three lab images: gate, web and the hosts on the plains.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>nftables, iproute2</td><td>Your firewall and routing in the gate.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>tcpdump, ping, curl, netcat</td><td>Your tools for seeing what happens on the network.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>nginx, socat, OpenSSH</td><td>The services on the notice board.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>curl, nmap, netcat</td><td>The scripted traffic from the plains: citizens, scans and floods.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>JSON</td><td>Scenario, themes and history (<code>~/.azeroth/history.json</code>).</td></tr>
                </table>
            </section>

            <section id="repo">
                <h2>The project</h2>
                <pre class="tree">lab/
  images/          the three lab images: gate, web, host
  scenarios/       sentinel-hill.json: waves, checks, hints, answers
themes/            theme-wow.json, theme-neutral.json
src/
  Game.Api/        web server, Blazor page, terminal
  Game.Core/       game master, sessions, themes, history
  Game.Checks/     traffic checks
  Game.Orchestration/  runs and the Docker client
